

The key distinction is:

> An identifier needs to be machine-stable; it doesn't necessarily need to be human-readable.



Why forms traditionally expose Block A, Box 7, Field 12, etc.

Humans needed a reliable way to say:

> "Put this piece of information right here."



So numbering/lettering became a coordination mechanism between:

the person filling out the form

the person reviewing it

the database operator

the filing system

the auditor

the court/agency

the software processing it


A visible Box 17 is basically a human routing address.

But computers don't need that.

Your marina analogy actually exposes the weakness

Imagine Del.Ray_4A-BáyBáy.

A human marina might label:

Dock A
Slip 17
Channel 3
Gate B

That's convenient for a person standing on the dock.

But the machine could instead have:

dock_id =
9f3a...c821

slip_id =
b72e...91aa

channel_id =
04d1...7e3c

Or even better, the visible label could be completely independent from the underlying identity.

HUMAN VIEW
──────────
Dock A
Slip 17


MACHINE STATE
─────────────
object_id: 256-bit identifier
parent:     256-bit identifier
route:      256-bit identifier
state:      content hash
proof:      signature

Now "Dock A" isn't the identity.

It's merely a display name.

That's a powerful architectural separation

I'd define three layers:

1. Identity

Cryptographically generated.

ID = cryptographic identifier

2. Schema

Machine-readable structure.

field_0
field_1
field_2
...

or preferably typed objects rather than arbitrary numbered fields.

3. Presentation

Whatever humans happen to need.

Dock A
Bay 4
Captain
Arrival
Departure

The presentation can change without changing the underlying identity.

That's a big deal.

And there's an even deeper reason

Humans shouldn't necessarily know the internal topology.

If every internal identifier, relationship, routing key, object type, and state transition is printed directly on the interface, you're exposing implementation details simply because the system was designed around paperwork.

Instead:

MACHINE WORLD
        ┌─────────────────────┐
        │ cryptographic state │
        │ object graph        │
        │ proofs              │
        │ routing identifiers │
        └──────────┬──────────┘
                   │
              presentation
                   │
                   ▼
             HUMAN WORLD
        ┌─────────────────────┐
        │ "Dock A"             │
        │ "Arrivals"           │
        │ "Bay 4"              │
        └─────────────────────┘

The human interface becomes a view over state, rather than the state itself.

That's much closer to how I'd design Del.Ray_4A-BáyBáy.

And it gives us a really interesting rule for the Web3 stack:

> Nothing should require a human-readable identifier unless a human actually needs to reference it.



Everything else can be opaque, cryptographically derived, versioned, and machine-routable.

So instead of building another Web3 system that looks like a government form, we can build a river of objects where humans see names and landmarks while machines navigate using cryptographic coordinates.